\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Sarah sat in the back of a limousine with a poncho across her knees and applied set of stick-on sideburns. She had fitted the black bouffant wig back at Sublime Point and set out at the earliest that baby and bladder could agree. Outside the old state parliament, she directed the driver to pull over. The limo slid as silent as a shark to the curb, and Sarah negotiated her belly out onto her feet, joining the walking crowd as one more fan among thousands.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 Soon after the ticket and bag checks, the music abruptly stopped mid-chorus. From her mis-spent nights in Elvi bars, she knew a fight must have broken out over a point of lyrical theology, and that the fight would be ugly.\
As she went with the crowd, she became aware of Elvi staring at her, and for more than just a thirty-five weeks bulge in a jumpsuit. She bit her lip and kept going, until a young Elvis blocked her path, went down on one knee in the trampled grass, and genuflected in the Elvi way, which is to say, he took off his sunglasses.\
\'93Holy Momma,\'94 he said reverently with head bowed.\
\'93Get up!\'94 Sarah hissed at him.\
The boy did not obey.\
\'93What\'92s your name?\'94\
\'93Walter.\'94\
\'93Arise, Walter,\'94 she said regally.\
Walter arose.\
Sarah resumed walking, dragging Walter by the shoulder. \'93Look, I\'92m a fan, just a very big fan.\'94\
\'93If you say so, Holy\'97\'94\
\'93Don\'92t call me that! Just \'91Sarah\'92, okay? Or Ms Jacobson, if you need to be offensively formal.\'94\
\'93Okay, Sarah.\'94\
Sarah was not convinced the kid would keep mum on the Holy Momma thing. She tugged him to a side alley between two merch tents. \'93Look. If you play your cards right, I can introduce you to the Great Teacher, all right?\'94\
\'93Really?\'94 Walter\'92s face brightened in a way only a face with those genetics can. \'93What about Aunt Kathy? She\'92s hot.\'94\
\'93Sure, Aunt Kathy. Why not? But you\'92ll have to behave or Aunt Hayley will put you in jail.\'94 Sarah looked about at the passing crowd. \'93Am I that easy to recognise?\'94\
Walter shuffled his feet. \'93Me and all my friends have posters on our bedroom walls\'97of you, dressed exactly like that.\'94 He looked at her distended jumpsuit. \'93But not pregnant.\'94\
\'93But with the hair?\'94\
Walter nodded.\
\'93And the sideburns?\'94\
\'93Well, they don\'92t look fake on the poster.\'94\
\'93And a jumpsuit?\'94\
Walter face twisted. \'93Not so much.\'94\
Sarah huffed. Having set out at the earliest that baby and bladder could agree, she was already exhausted and in need of a loo. Deflated, she removed the poor disguise and let her blonde hair flow. Walter looked disappointed.\
Two volunteer marshals appeared.\
\'93Holy Momma,\'94 one of them said.\
\'93Boys,\'94 she answered.\
\'93If you would like to come with us, your throne is prepared.\'94\
Sarah resigned herself to it. 
\f2\i What next? 
\f1\i0 she wondered. 
\f2\i Would Eloise show up and demand she get back to work?
\f1\i0 \
\'93Okay,\'94 she said. \'93But Walter\'92s coming too.\'94\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 The VIP platform stood mid-field in front of the engineering tower, under which were a couple of heavily guarded portaloos.   Sarah smiled and rushed them before anyone could stop her.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 After an extended period, in which Walter was convinced someone would shoo him off, Sarah reemerged.\
\'93Come on,\'94 she said to Walter as she took the stairs to the platform, which itself was embellished, to say the least. Its scaffolding wore shag carpet and the shag wore bunting. The platform was shagged too. Above and behind, the tower held desks and racks for sound and lights, and half a dozen people were up there either twiddling things or standing with their arms crossed like they\'92d seen it all before. Under the canopy were two thrones now on their third career\'97burlesque first, then the Inauguration, now this. Sarah\'92s throne was nearest the stairs, which she connected to the guarded toilets as was very thankful. Aunt Kathy and Aunt Hayley sat in two ordinary folding chairs beyond the thrones, because in the theology of the Elvi the Holy Momma and the Great Teacher sat enthroned, but aunts sat like everybody else.\
The stage dead ahead, squared off against the platform and the right size, thousands of people between, with the sound arriving whole, mixed, finished, like a well-spiced meal she hadn\'92t seen anyone cook.\
Kathy, dressed in tie-dyed lab coat over festival glitter, rose and came across the carpet, with her arms out and a cocktail in each hand, which made the hug a feat of part affection, part gymnastics; Hayley stayed in her chair nearest the far rail and gave Sarah a nod that was, for Hayley, a ticker-tape parade.\
\'93We had a picnic blanket,\'94 Kathy said, her voice raised over the music. \'93Lovely spot. Safe way back by the fig trees, you could hear yourself think. And then a nice young man with sideburns came and apologised and \'91offered\'92 us better seats.\'94\
Sarah nodded, \'93Yes, I got a similar offer.\'94 Then she introduced the boggled and enraptured Walter to the aunts, and warned Kathy, \'93Don\'92t give the kid any patches.\'94\
Sarah considered the throne\'97not 
\f2\i her
\f1\i0  throne\'97just some intestine-crushing ceremonial rack they made her sit in, with gilt trimmings and oceanic velvet. She sat in instalments, the way one might lower a piano, while Kathy supervised progress with both hands out and her lips moving. The velvet returned the day's heat with interest, mostly through the backs of Sarah\'92s knees.\
Kathy took her seat, and pat Chanta\'92s throne. \'93Come sit here, Walter.\'94\
\'93Me?\'94\
\'93Only till The Great Teacher turns up.\'94\
\'93Wow! Thanks.\'94 Walter took to the throne and sat like he owned it, though he couldn\'92t believe he was sitting between the Holy Momma and Aunt Kathy. Kathy offered to take a selfie with him, which just turned his day up to eleven, until things settled down a little and he caught Kathy and Haley holding hands.\
Onstage the band finished its number, and the frontman\'97in a cape the colour of a swimming pool\'97held one hand up against the applause and shaded his eyes toward the platform with the other.\
\'93Friends,\'94 he called, "the Holy Momma has arrived!\'94\
Everyone in front turned. The whole arena cheered. Sarah lifted a hand and waved. Walter stood up, saluted and took the adulation like he was a politician. Or an entertainer, which he almost certainly was destined to be.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Walter had been observing a floating object casting a shadow over the crowd and, a few songs later, after seeing it was moored to the tower by a cable as thick as his wrist, asked Kathy about it. Sarah listened for the answer too.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 \'93That\'92s Colonel Tom Porker,\'94 Kathy said.\
\'93It\'92s a pig,\'94 Walter stated.\
Kathy nodded. It was true. Directly overhead floated a gassed-up dirigible of a pig the size of a delivery van, upholstered in a saturated pink that would embarrass a Malaysian dessert franchise.\
\'93Wearing a big hat.\'94\
\'93It\'92s a Stetson,\'94 Kathy elaborated though, to be completely accurate, she\'92d have noted that the Stetson would\'92ve fit a much larger pig.\
\'93And it\'92s smoking a cigar.\'94\
The cigar jutting from the pig\'92s mouth at what Kathy judged to be too close to average erection angle was, in fact, puffing rings from what she guessed must be an internal fog unit.\
\'93Right all counts, Walter. Go to the top of the class.\'94\
Walter liked that. He\'92d never been the top anything, so going to the top of the class.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Not that she expected him to, but Sarah was sad Chanta hadn\'92t shown up all afternoon. She quizzed Kathy, who reluctantly gave up that Chanta was in the hospitality tent among the musos, and even more reluctantly agreed that he was slightly more among the Elvisettes that had infested the back stage area. \
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 \'93Rider\'92s better back there,\'94 Hayley said from the rail, her eyes scanning the crowd for trouble, even while snuggling Kathy.\
Sarah drew her inferences, and animated her imagination accordingly. Sarah kept her eyes off the throne of The Great Teacher the way you keep your tongue off a cracked tooth. Walter had long vacated it, and was standing at the platform\'92s rail attracting attention and waving at friends.\
The punch-ups subsided after the High Elvis laid down the law and, anyway, the later bands were more familiar with the words of The Great Teacher. Cocktail hour came and went, played out by a nine-piece band with two drummers and a misappropriated 
\f2\i yidaki
\f1\i0 , while the sun melted down among the fig trees and made the Eastern Suburbs Bubbles opalescent. The crowd lit up with thousands of tablet screens and the odd contraband sparkler. Sarah drank juice, shifted her posture against under-padded throne, and let the noise vibrate into her pelvis and jangled vertebrae. The bands had been getting better all since the fighting stopped; past sundown, they resonated with memories of a night which might have marked the last time Sarah was happy, at her first Vegas Show. As then, she felt the music pulse in her ribs, the warmth of brass sections, the air thickening the way it had at Graceland when a soloist stepped to a microphone and undid her, and she had wept and felt herself, for the first time, one filament in a braiding of love that didn\'92t end anywhere. She had fallen asleep that night glowing. The memory of the music then and live performance before her re-associated her with that original state of grace before the Sages had helped themselves to her future. She was still experiencing the music of two events in one present skin when an office chair arrived on the platform.\
The chair was borne by a young man whose own tailoring suggested a working life spent adjacent to greatness. He set it down beside Sarah\'92s throne, squared it to the stage with a surveyor\'92s care, and withdrew. Sarah regarded the chair, parked there, as it was, with the serene incongruity of a bank vault attending a picnic. Sarah recognised the brand, Ermine Muller, and guessed with a side of dread who it would be for.\
Eloise came up the stairs, and sat without making eye contact with Sarah or, indeed anyone on the platform. Wearing an understated business ensemble that was worth more than a small African nation\'92s defence budget, she merely sat with perfect posture in an office chair that most of the audience wold need to sign a kidney away to possess, and held the stage in her gaze as if she was meditating over a ballet in which absolutely nothing would ever happen. Sarah was relieved there was no dentist-assassin entourage, which gave her hope there would be no executions for the mere purpose of making a point.\
\'93Hello, Granny,\'94 Walter said from the front corner of the platform beyond Hayley\'92s seat.\
Sarah held her breath and recalculated the odds of imminent death.\
Eloise peered into the swirl of darkness and stage lighting. \'93Hello, urchin,\'94 she said and smiled for the kid. Then went back to watching the non-existent ballet.\
Sarah waited. There was no reproach. There was no remark. Nothing about the security risk of being out here without a Shakti Enterprises security detail. Nothing about telling Eloise she was going out, as family who respected each other would   do.\
\'93It would have been nice if you had invited me,\'94 was all Eloise said, but she had said less before and still managed to threaten calamity for all within earshot.\
\'93What, do you think you\'92re just one of the girls?\'94\
Eloise laughed. \'93Is the notion so scandalous? Do you think I can just call up my arms-dealer friends and arrange a night at the pub?\'94\
Kathy leaned across Sarah and offered Eloise a patch. \'93It\'92ll soak the harsh out,\'94 she explained.\
Eloise took the offering noncommittally and stowed it in her handbag.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Eloise lasted until the remainder of the current band\'92s set before she pulled out her tablet and started working.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 \'93Please tell me she doesn\'92t have anything incoming that\'92s aimed at the stage,\'94 Kathy said to Sarah.\
Even with the crowd noise, Eloise heard. \'93You should ask Hayley,\'94 she said. \'93She\'92s the Chief Security Officer here.\'94\
Hayley leaned forward and stared at Eloise.\
Eloise shrugged. \'93I have no missiles aimed at the stage. The Bubble would only frazzle them out and cook everyone underneath. Including me. And my grandchild.\'94 To everyone on the platform\'97perhaps even Walter\'97the way Eloise referred to Sarah\'92s baby sounded like she was speaking about a transaction.\
Overhead, Colonel Tom Porker puffed his rings at the stars.
